1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,395
Microsoft Foundry: from
prompt to production agent.

2
00:00:03,445 --> 00:00:07,314
In the next few minutes, we'll
walk through Foundry Guide,

3
00:00:07,364 --> 00:00:14,880
a small web app running on Azure Container Apps and backed
by a prompt agent in Microsoft Foundry Agent Service.

4
00:00:14,930 --> 00:00:20,420
You'll see grounded answers, real code
execution, a side-by-side model comparison,

5
00:00:20,470 --> 00:00:21,501
and run history.

6
00:00:21,551 --> 00:00:23,190
And all of it is keyless.

7
00:00:24,118 --> 00:00:26,600
Let's start with the architecture.

8
00:00:26,650 --> 00:00:33,229
A Vue front end and a FastAPI back end run in
a single container on Azure Container Apps.

9
00:00:33,279 --> 00:00:38,965
The back end calls our Foundry project
with a user-assigned managed identity.

10
00:00:39,015 --> 00:00:41,274
There are no API keys anywhere,

11
00:00:41,324 --> 00:00:45,817
and local authentication is
disabled on the Foundry resource.

12
00:00:45,867 --> 00:00:51,850
On the right are the live deployment
details: the project runs in Sweden Central,

13
00:00:51,900 --> 00:00:57,213
with gpt-5.4-mini as the agent model
and gpt-5.4-nano as the fast model.

14
00:00:57,263 --> 00:01:02,427
The foundry-guide agent has two tools:
the Microsoft Learn MCP server,

15
00:01:02,477 --> 00:01:03,991
and Code Interpreter.

16
00:01:04,041 --> 00:01:08,311
Traces flow to Application
Insights through OpenTelemetry.

17
00:01:09,271 --> 00:01:10,251
Now, the agent.

18
00:01:10,301 --> 00:01:13,889
I'll ask a question that
needs current documentation:

19
00:01:13,939 --> 00:01:19,930
what is the difference between prompt agents
and hosted agents in Foundry Agent Service,

20
00:01:19,980 --> 00:01:22,058
with Microsoft Learn citations.

21
00:01:22,108 --> 00:01:26,727
The agent decides on its own to call
the Microsoft Learn MCP server.

22
00:01:26,777 --> 00:01:30,777
That server is public, read-only,
and needs no credentials,

23
00:01:30,827 --> 00:01:33,866
so we let the agent call it without approval.

24
00:01:33,916 --> 00:01:37,230
For any tool that writes
data, keep approvals on.

25
00:01:38,191 --> 00:01:39,371
Here's the answer.

26
00:01:39,421 --> 00:01:44,907
Prompt agents combine instructions, a
model, and tools, fully managed by Foundry.

27
00:01:44,957 --> 00:01:49,623
Hosted agents run your own code and
framework in a managed container.

28
00:01:49,673 --> 00:01:54,339
Look at the chips at the top: these
are the MCP calls the agent made.

29
00:01:54,389 --> 00:01:57,620
And the links point back to learn.microsoft.com.

30
00:01:57,670 --> 00:01:59,124
Grounded, not guessed.

31
00:01:59,174 --> 00:02:03,908
Underneath, you can see the latency
and the token usage for this turn.

32
00:02:04,823 --> 00:02:05,736
Next, numbers.

33
00:02:05,786 --> 00:02:11,103
Language models are unreliable at
arithmetic, so the agent's instructions say:

34
00:02:11,153 --> 00:02:13,924
use Code Interpreter for any calculation.

35
00:02:13,974 --> 00:02:18,052
I'm asking for the cost of two
thousand conversations a day,

36
00:02:18,102 --> 00:02:22,868
each with fifteen hundred input tokens
and five hundred output tokens,

37
00:02:22,918 --> 00:02:30,436
at illustrative prices of forty cents per million input
tokens and one dollar sixty per million output tokens.

38
00:02:30,486 --> 00:02:34,977
These are not list prices, so always
check the Azure pricing page.

39
00:02:35,027 --> 00:02:38,623
The agent now writes Python
and runs it in a sandbox.

40
00:02:39,526 --> 00:02:42,799
And here is the result,
computed rather than guessed:

41
00:02:42,849 --> 00:02:47,502
two dollars and eighty cents per day,
eighty-four dollars over thirty days,

42
00:02:47,552 --> 00:02:50,762
and one thousand and twenty-two
dollars over a year.

43
00:02:50,812 --> 00:02:53,897
The Code Interpreter chip
shows the tool that ran,

44
00:02:53,947 --> 00:02:59,352
and the bar chart is an image generated in
the sandbox and served back through the app.

45
00:02:59,402 --> 00:03:01,860
This is the same conversation as before:

46
00:03:01,910 --> 00:03:05,371
Foundry keeps the conversation
state on the server side.

47
00:03:06,232 --> 00:03:09,500
Which model should power an agent like this?

48
00:03:09,550 --> 00:03:10,556
Let's measure.

49
00:03:10,606 --> 00:03:12,441
On the Model compare tab,

50
00:03:12,491 --> 00:03:18,927
the same prompt goes to two Foundry deployments
in parallel through the Responses API:

51
00:03:18,977 --> 00:03:21,265
gpt-5.4-mini, and gpt-5.4-nano.

52
00:03:21,315 --> 00:03:23,452
The prompt: in three bullets,

53
00:03:23,502 --> 00:03:28,052
explain why teams put an AI
gateway in front of their models.

54
00:03:28,916 --> 00:03:34,850
Two answers, side by side, each with its
latency and its input and output tokens,

55
00:03:34,900 --> 00:03:37,658
and a trophy for the fastest response.

56
00:03:37,708 --> 00:03:41,278
For many tasks, the smaller
model is good enough.

57
00:03:41,328 --> 00:03:45,785
The only way to know is to
measure, ideally with evaluations.

58
00:03:46,669 --> 00:03:51,922
Every call we just made lands in Run
history: the time, the kind of run,

59
00:03:51,972 --> 00:03:56,342
the agent or model, latency,
tokens, tool calls, and status.

60
00:03:56,392 --> 00:04:01,056
I can sort by latency, or search
for just the model comparisons.

61
00:04:01,106 --> 00:04:03,487
It's kept in memory for the demo.

62
00:04:03,537 --> 00:04:09,748
In production, the same signals live in
Application Insights as OpenTelemetry traces.

63
00:04:10,522 --> 00:04:14,474
To recap: a prompt agent in
Microsoft Foundry Agent Service,

64
00:04:14,524 --> 00:04:19,610
grounded in Microsoft Learn through MCP,
doing real math in Code Interpreter,

65
00:04:19,660 --> 00:04:24,012
called keylessly from Azure Container
Apps, with every run traced.

66
00:04:24,062 --> 00:04:26,813
The source code, the Bicep infrastructure,

67
00:04:26,863 --> 00:04:32,349
and the GitHub Actions pipeline are all in
the repository linked on the About page.

68
00:04:32,399 --> 00:04:33,683
Thanks for watching.
